message.content:
{
  "answer_markdown": "No, Mistral's raw endpoint on **Google Cloud** does not inherently remember recently used prompts to save time, and there is no built-in mechanism that reduces costs for requests reusing cached content [1].\n\n### Key Points:\n- **Prompt caching** is a feature available on Mistral's API (e.g., `mistral.ai/v1/chat/completions`), not on Google Cloud deployments like Cerebrium [1][4].\n- To enable prompt caching, you must explicitly set a `prompt_cache_key` in your API request, which allows Mistral to reuse shared prompt prefixes across requests [1].\n- **Google Cloud deployments** (e.g., via Cerebrium) do not mention prompt caching or cost savings for reused prompts in the provided documentation [4].\n- The raw endpoint on Google Cloud treats each request independently, with no automatic caching or cost reduction for repeated prompts [4].\n\nIf you deploy Mistral via **Cerebrium on Google Cloud**, you must implement caching logic yourself (e.g., client-side or application-level caching) [4].",
  "citations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "Use `prompt_cache_key` for multi-turn conversations, repeated system prompts, or workloads that send similar context across requests"
    },
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "Set the same `prompt_cache_key` on requests that are likely to share a prefix"
    },
    {
      "n": 4,
      "quote": "You will see your application deploy, install pip packages and download the model. Once completed it will output a CURL request you can use to call your endpoint"
    },
    {
      "n": 4,
      "quote": "curl --location --request POST 'https://api.cortex.cerebrium.ai/v4/p-<YOUR PROJECT ID>/mistral-vllm/run'"
    }
  ],
  "insufficient_evidence": false
}

